// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// Docs only: AS3b, the executor interface. 20 anchored edits over 8 files (the async design record,
// parked ideas, the CLI doc, the site CLI page, the async guide, two site pages that still said there are no
// threads, project status and the README). The code is in the tarball as3b-executor-replacements.tar.gz. Each
// anchor occurs exactly once in the files as they are on master and none is a whole line. This overwrites the
// earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there is only ever one (the log entry it held is applied on master).

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "threads when AS3a landed, 2026-11-19)", "threads when AS3a landed, 2026-11-19, the executor when AS3b landed, 2026-11-21)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- `Task.spawn` and `join` exist (AS3a, 2026-11-19). No `Mutex`", "- `Task.spawn` and `join` exist (AS3a, 2026-11-19). The pool runs on an `Executor` that a host can\n  replace, and the worker count is a setting (AS3b, 2026-11-21). No `Mutex`")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "### AS3a decisions (2026-11-19)\n\n- Decided with the user: the interpreter keeps `Rc` inside each task", "### AS3b decisions (2026-11-21)\n\n- Decided with the user: the executor interface is \"the host lends threads or fibers\". The runtime\n  keeps the task queue and builds the interpreters; the host decides which threads or fibers run\n  the worker loops and the program's own `main`, and how a waiting caller parks and wakes (item 3,\n  as recommended; the alternative was per-task job submission, where the host runs one job for each\n  task and never sees a worker loop). The worker count is set by the `--workers` flag of `ubel run`,\n  by the `UBEL_WORKERS` environment variable when the flag is absent, and through the API\n  (`Interpreter::set_worker_count`), as recommended. A worker that waits for a task blocks. If the\n  task is still queued it runs it itself, and it does not run other queued tasks while it waits (as\n  recommended).\n- Settled without a question:\n  - The interface is the `Executor` trait in `interpreter/executor.rs`: `run(Lend)`,\n    `park_until(ready)`, `wake()` and `preferred_workers()`. A `Lend` carries the number of workers,\n    the worker loop, a stop function and the program's `main`. A host starts that many workers\n    (`lend.worker()(i)` on a thread or fiber each), calls `lend.run_main()` once on the calling\n    thread, calls `lend.stop()` and returns when the workers have returned. `run` blocks, and the\n    thread that calls it is the one that runs `main`. `run_main` panics if it is called twice.\n  - `preferred_workers()` is an addition to the interface sketched in 2.8: a host that knows how\n    many threads it owns says so. The count a run uses is the explicit setting, else the host's\n    preference, else the logical cores minus one (at least one). Zero is a count: the program runs\n    with no workers, the host is not called, and `Task.spawn` reports that there are none.\n  - Every wait of the pool goes through the executor, the main thread's included. A worker with no\n    task and a thread that awaits a spawned task call `park_until`; queuing a task, ending one and\n    shutting the pool down each call `wake`. The pool has no condition variable of its own. The\n    default executor parks callers on one condition variable and wakes all of them on each `wake`.\n  - A host that cannot start a worker calls `stop()` for the ones it did start and returns an\n    error. `main` has not run then, and the error text is the reason the program did not start. The\n    default executor also stops the workers when `main` panics, so its thread scope cannot wait for\n    ever.\n  - `WORKER_STACK_BYTES` (128 MiB) moved from the pool to `stack.rs` and is documented as the\n    minimum stack of any thread or fiber a host lends. Nothing checks it.\n  - `--workers` belongs to `ubel run` alone. The flag wins over `UBEL_WORKERS`, also when the\n    variable is not a number; an empty or blank variable counts as unset; a variable that is not a\n    number is a usage error (exit code 2) that names it and its value.\n- Known gaps, recorded in `docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md`: the stack a host gives a worker is not checked, the\n  default executor wakes every parked caller on each wake, the executor sees no task, `run` blocks\n  and the caller runs `main`, and the native backend has no executor.\n\n### AS3a decisions (2026-11-19)\n\n- Decided with the user: the interpreter keeps `Rc` inside each task")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "The default worker count and thread configuration are open (section 5, item 3).", "Built in AS3b (2026-11-21): the interface is the `Executor` trait (`interpreter/executor.rs`), and\n  the host lends threads or fibers to the pool instead of receiving one job per task. The runtime\n  keeps the queue and the worker interpreters; the host runs the worker loops and the program's\n  `main`, parks a caller that has to wait and wakes it when the pool changes state. The default\n  executor gives each worker an operating-system thread. The default worker count (the logical\n  cores minus one, at least one) is replaced by `--workers`, `UBEL_WORKERS`,\n  `Interpreter::set_worker_count` or `Executor::preferred_workers`. Thread names and affinity are\n  not part of the interface: an engine that pins threads starts the threads itself.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "threads brings its own executor. The\n   interface itself is AS3b.", "threads brings its own executor.\n   *Decided 2026-11-21 and built in AS3b:* the host lends threads or fibers to the pool, the count\n   is set by `--workers`, `UBEL_WORKERS` and the API, and a waiting worker blocks, runs its own\n   awaited task when that is still queued and runs no other queued task, all as recommended. See\n   \"AS3b decisions\" in section 0.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "| **AS3b** | The executor interface, a replaceable pool, the park hook, the worker-count setting.", "| **AS3b** | **Done 2026-11-21.** The executor interface (`Executor` and `Lend`: the host lends threads or fibers, and every wait and wake of the pool goes through it), a pool that runs on it, the park hook and the worker-count setting (`--workers`, `UBEL_WORKERS`, the API).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md", "- `Task.spawn` is refused in a run with no workers. The browser playground has none.\n\n## Built\n", "- `Task.spawn` is refused in a run with no workers. The browser playground has none.\n\n## Found while building AS3b (parked)\n\n- Nothing checks the stack a host gives a worker. `WORKER_STACK_BYTES` is documented as the minimum,\n  but the runtime cannot read the size of the thread or fiber that calls the worker loop. A host\n  with small stacks overflows on a deep recursion inside a task, which ends the process and is not\n  an error the program can see.\n- The default executor wakes every parked caller on each `wake`. With many idle workers, queuing one\n  task wakes all of them and all but one go back to sleep. A wake that says what changed (a task\n  queued, a task ended) or one condition variable for each waiter would avoid it. For a handful of\n  workers the cost is small.\n- The executor sees no task. A host cannot give one task a priority, pin it to a core or keep the\n  tasks of one job together, because it only runs worker loops. Per-task job submission was the\n  alternative that was not chosen (`docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`, \"AS3b decisions\"); a host that needs\n  that would need a second method on the interface.\n- `Executor::run` blocks, and the thread that calls it runs `main`. A host whose frame loop owns the\n  main thread cannot start the program and return to its loop. A fiber host that runs a worker on the\n  same thread as `main` would also share that thread's output capture, which is attached per thread.\n- The native backend has no executor. How its runtime lends threads is part of AS8.\n\n## Built\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel_stratum_cli.md", "reports it (`check::report` is shared).", "reports it (`check::report` is shared).\n\n`--workers N` sets the number of worker threads for a program that spawns tasks (`Task.spawn`).\nWithout the flag the `UBEL_WORKERS` environment variable is read, and without that the runtime\nuses the logical cores minus one, at least one. `resolve_workers` settles it: the flag wins even\nwhen the variable is not a number, an empty or blank variable is unset, the value is trimmed, zero\nis a count (the program then has no workers and `Task.spawn` says so), and a variable that is not a\nnumber is a usage error (exit code 2) that names `UBEL_WORKERS` and the value. A program that never\nspawns ignores the setting. The flag belongs to `run` alone.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel_stratum_cli.md", "cannot know that `let x: u8 = 5` holds a `u8`.", "cannot know that `let x: u8 = 5` holds a `u8`.\n`run_file_with(path, workers)` is `run_file` with a worker count handed to\n`Interpreter::set_worker_count` before `run_program`; `run_file` calls it with `None`.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/tooling/cli.md", "stratum: 7 letters\n```\n\n## `ubel build <file>`", "stratum: 7 letters\n```\n\nA program that starts tasks with `Task.spawn` runs them on a pool of worker threads. The pool has\none thread fewer than the machine has logical cores, and at least one. `--workers N` sets another\nnumber, and the `UBEL_WORKERS` environment variable does the same when the flag is absent; the flag\nwins. `--workers 0` runs the program with no workers, and `Task.spawn` then reports that there are\nnone. A program that never calls `Task.spawn` starts no thread and ignores both. A value that is not\na whole number is a usage error and exits with `2`.\n\n```text\n$ ubel run --workers 4 crawl.ubl\n$ UBEL_WORKERS=2 ubel run crawl.ubl\n```\n\n## `ubel build <file>`")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "works in every tier. There are no threads yet: a task runs inline where it is awaited, so\n> nothing here runs in parallel. Parallelism is the next stage of a larger design, summarised below.", "works in every tier. A task that is only awaited runs inline where it is awaited, and\n> `Task.spawn` runs one on a pool of worker threads (see below). Sharing data between tasks is the\n> next stage of a larger design, summarised below.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "The pool has one worker fewer than the machine has logical cores, and at least one. There\nare no threads in the browser playground, so `Task.spawn` reports an error there.", "The pool has one worker fewer than the machine has logical cores, and at least one. `ubel run\n--workers N` or the `UBEL_WORKERS` environment variable sets another number, and a program that\nembeds the interpreter calls `set_worker_count`. There are no threads in the browser playground, so\n`Task.spawn` reports an error there.\n\n### Who runs the workers\n\nThe pool does not start threads itself. It hands the worker loops, and the program's own `main`, to\nan `Executor`, which decides where they run. The default executor gives every worker an operating\nsystem thread. An engine that owns its threads, to pin them to cores or to run the workers as fibers\nof its job system, brings an executor of its own and gives it to the interpreter with\n`set_executor`. Every wait of the pool goes through the executor (`park_until`), and so does every\nchange a waiter could be waiting for (`wake`), so a fiber host can park a fiber where a thread host\nwould block a thread. An executor may also say how many workers it wants (`preferred_workers`); an\nexplicit `--workers` or `UBEL_WORKERS` count wins over that. A thread or fiber that runs a worker\nneeds at least 128 MiB of stack, which the runtime cannot check.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "tasks on real threads are built (see", "tasks on real threads, on a pool an engine can replace, are built (see")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "continues with the sharing rule, the lock types and the executor an engine can replace. [Project Status]", "continues with the sharing rule and the lock types. [Project Status]")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/errors-and-optionals.md", "There\nare no threads yet, so a task runs where it is awaited. See", "A\ntask that is only awaited runs where it is awaited, and `Task.spawn` runs one on a worker thread. See")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "waits. Next: the sharing rule and the lock types", "waits. The pool runs on an executor interface (2026-11-21): a host can lend its own\n  threads or fibers, and `ubel run --workers N` or `UBEL_WORKERS` sets the worker count. Next: the\n  sharing rule and the lock types")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "There are no threads, locks, atomics or channels,", "There are no locks, atomics or channels,")
    patch_replace_text("README.md", "and `Task.spawn` on a pool of worker threads, where a value", "and `Task.spawn` on a pool of worker threads that an engine can replace through an executor interface, where a value")
    patch_replace_text("README.md", "scopes, cancellation and an executor an engine can replace are **designed, not built**", "scopes and cancellation are **designed, not built**")
    patch_replace_text("README.md", "playground has no threads, so `Task.spawn` reports an error there. How tasks\nwill share data safely is the next piece of design work.", "playground has no threads, so `Task.spawn` reports an error there.\n`ubel run --workers N` (or the `UBEL_WORKERS` environment variable) sets how many worker\nthreads the pool has. How tasks will share data safely is the next piece of design work.")
)
